{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need count of satisfying assignments for CNF with 12 variables. Clauses list:\n\n1. [-9,1] => ¬x9 ∨ x1\n2. [-2,3] => ¬x2 ∨ x3\n3. [4,12] => x4 ∨ x12\n4. [-6,7] => ¬x6 ∨ x7\n5. [-5,1] => ¬x5 ∨ x1\n6. [-11,3] => ¬x11 ∨ x3\n7. [9,5] => x9 ∨ x5\n8. [6,10] => x6 ∨ x10\n9. [-10,7] => ¬x10 ∨ x7\n10. [11,2] => x11 ∨ x2\n11. [-4,8] => ¬x4 ∨ x8\n12. [-12,8] => ¬x12 ∨ x8\n\nWe need count of assignments satisfying all. Let's analyze constraints.\n\nWe have variables 1-12.\n\nClauses pairwise:\n\nClause1: ¬x9 ∨ x1 => x9 -> x1. Equivalent: if x9=1 then x1=1. If x9=0 no restriction.\n\nClause5: ¬x5 ∨ x1 => x5 -> x1. So if x5=1 then x1=1.\n\nClause7: x9 ∨ x5 => at least one of x9,x5 is 1.\n\nThus combine: at least one of x9,x5 is 1, and if either is 1 then x1 must be 1. So x1 must be 1 if (x9=1 or x5=1). But it's possible both x9 and x5 are 0? No, clause7 forbids both 0. So at least one is 1, thus x1 must be 1. So x1=1 forced.\n\nThus x1=1.\n\nNow clause2: ¬x2 ∨ x3 => x2 -> x3. Clause10: x11 ∨ x2 => at least one of x11,x2 is 1. Clause6: ¬x11 ∨ x3 => x11 -> x3. So we have implications: x2->x3, x11->x3. Also at least one of x11,x2 is 1. So either x2=1 or x11=1 (or both). In either case, x3 must be 1. So x3=1 forced.\n\nThus x3=1.\n\nNow clause3: x4 ∨ x12 => at least one of x4,x12 is 1.\n\nClause11: ¬x4 ∨ x8 => x4 -> x8. Clause12: ¬x12 ∨ x8 => x12 -> x8. So if either x4 or x12 is 1, then x8 must be 1. Since at least one of x4,x12 is 1, x8 must be 1. So x8=1 forced.\n\nClause4: ¬x6 ∨ x7 => x6 -> x7. Clause8: x6 ∨ x10 => at least one of x6,x10 is 1. Clause9: ¬x10 ∨ x7 => x10 -> x7. So we have: at least one of x6,x10 is 1, and if either is 1 then x7 must be 1. Thus x7=1 forced.\n\nThus x7=1.\n\n"}}],"created":1790948338,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-09-10\\napraw\\outputs\\moon-native\\models\\gpt-oss-20b-MXFP4.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10809-5266f24da"